{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Don't reach for a ref-flag workaround</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A common "fix" is a ref-based flag that skips the effect's second run in development. Don't do this, it silences the warning without fixing the underlying bug, and the same bug will resurface the moment the component genuinely remounts for an unrelated reason in production.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>fetch</code> calls inside effects need the same treatment: use an <code>AbortController</code> in the cleanup to cancel an in-flight request, rather than letting a stale response from the first invocation land after the second invocation has already started its own request.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Strict Mode's double-invoke is dev-only and doesn't run in production builds at all, so an effect that "passes" by never being tested under it can still hide a real production bug, the double-invoke is a detector, not the actual risk. Treat any Strict Mode warning as a genuine correctness bug to fix, not a development-environment quirk to route around.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

